===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|aɪt|lang=en}}
* {{IPA|/haɪt/|lang=en}}
* {{homophones|height|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} {{m|enm|hight||to be named, be called}} (alternative past participle of {{m|enm|hoten}}), from {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|hēht||tr=preterite of {{m|ang|hātan}}|to be named, be called}}, from *''hehait-'', reduplicate preterite base of {{etyl|gem-pro|en}} {{m|gem-pro|*haitaną||to call, command, summon}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|en}} {{m|ine-pro|*key(w)-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*kyew-||to set in motion}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|heten}}, {{cog|de|heißen}}, {{cog|da|hedde}}, {{cog|fy|hite}}, {{cog|nl|heten}}, and {{cog|sv|heta}}, {{cog|la|cieō||I call, I set in motion}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|-|-|hight}}
''hight'' is only the [[preterite]] or past participle, not the infinitive or present.
# {{lb|en|archaic|transitive}} To [[call]], [[name]].
#* Byron
#*: Childe Harold was he '''hight'''.
# {{lb|en|archaic|intransitive}} To be called or named.
#* Surrey
#*: Bright was her hue, and Geraldine she '''hight'''.

